{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-71814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }x<y<{x}^{2},\\,1<x\\le 2}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }2<y<x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }4\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063635/state.json" "file_sha256" "825d1609e89d5a6fb58051d5ce9d3821f40d79bad1999807cae8bbf598fb5f75"} {"question" "q-71814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(2,2).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063635/state.json" "file_sha256" "825d1609e89d5a6fb58051d5ce9d3821f40d79bad1999807cae8bbf598fb5f75"} {"question" "q-71814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home